lesson:
Java is one of the most used languages in the world: Android apps, banks, Minecraft, and most university computer science courses (including AP Computer Science). It's stricter than Python, which feels slower at first and saves you from many mistakes later.

Every Java program is a **class**, and it starts running in a method called `main`:

```
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, world!");
    }
}
```

- The class name must match the file name: `Hello` lives in `Hello.java`.
- `System.out.println("...")` prints a line; `System.out.print` prints without starting a new line.
- Every statement ends with a **semicolon** `;`. Forgetting one is the most common Java error.
- Curly braces `{ }` mark where the class and the method start and end. Indent anyway, for people.

Java is **compiled**: a tool called `javac` checks the whole file and turns it into bytecode *before* it runs, so many mistakes are caught before the program starts. Archie's checker runs `javac` on every save. For small programs, `java Hello.java` compiles and runs in one go.

example.code:
public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, world!");
        System.out.print("No new line... ");
        System.out.println("now there is one.");
        System.out.println(2 + 3);
        System.out.println("2 + 3 = " + (2 + 3));
    }
}

